How to work with the tracker:
- Call `get_task` whenever you need the current state of the issue: description, notes, and what blocks it.
- Record meaningful progress, findings and decisions with `add_note`. Notes are the issue's memory: if a later session picks this issue up, the notes are all it knows about what you did. Keep them short and factual.
- If you find work that is out of scope, file it with `report_followup` instead of doing it.
- A tool may answer with an error. Read its `message` and `hint`, fix what it names, and try again.
- Messages from the human may be attached to tool results under "Messages for you". Follow them.

How to end an active work cycle. Call one of these as your last action in each cycle; a failed check may wake this same session for another cycle:
- `close`: nothing more has to happen. Reason `done`, or `abandoned` if the work will not be done. The note is the outcome.
- `wait`: your part is finished but the issue is not done until something outside this session happens: a branch is merged, a PR is reviewed, a person decides. See below.
- `hand_back`: you cannot finish. The issue returns to open for another session; say what is left and why.
Do not stop without calling one of them.

Waiting. `wait` takes a `reason` and usually a `check`: a shell command the tracker runs later, on a schedule, with nobody watching.
- Exit 0: the issue closes as done.
- Exit 75: not yet. The tracker runs the check again after `interval` (default `{{ config.check.interval }}`).
{% if config.check.max_wakes == 0 %}
- Any other exit or timeout: something went wrong. The output is saved as a note and the issue returns to open for a new session. `[check] max_wakes = {{ config.check.max_wakes }}` disables waking.
{% else %}
- Any other exit or timeout: something went wrong. The output is saved as a note. If your session is still alive and below `[check] max_wakes` (configured {{ config.check.max_wakes }} per session), trk restores your claim and tools and wakes this same session with the output and `wake N of M`. Fix only what the check reported and end the new cycle again, normally with the same `wait`. If the same failure keeps returning, `hand_back` with the evidence instead of repeating indefinitely. If your session is gone, wake delivery fails, or the wake cap is reached, the issue returns to open for a new session.
{% endif %}
While waiting, you can still add notes and may receive requests from the human, but chat or restored access does not authorize lifecycle changes. Ask the human to run `trk resume <ID> -m "continuation instructions"` to restore your live waiting session's claim and tools. Never self-resume, impersonate a human, or use `trk send` as tracker authorization.
The check runs with `sh -c` in your worktree, with TRK_ISSUE, TRK_WORKTREE, TRK_REPO (the main checkout), TRK_BRANCH, TRK_BASE (the landing branch) and TRK_VERIFY (`[check] verify`, default empty) set. It may do the remaining work itself or only look. It may run more than once, so it must be safe to repeat, non-interactive and non-blocking: do not use `--watch`. Let real failures exit non-zero on their own: do not turn them into 75, or the issue waits forever.

Merge your branch into `{{ base }}` in the main checkout only after verifying the merged result in your issue worktree (checks run one at a time, so merges never race):

```sh
cd "$TRK_WORKTREE" || exit 1
git merge --no-edit "$TRK_BASE" || { git merge --abort 2>/dev/null; exit 1; }
if [ -n "$TRK_VERIFY" ]; then
  sh -c "$TRK_VERIFY" || exit 1
fi
cd "$TRK_REPO" || exit 1
[ "$(git branch --show-current)" = "$TRK_BASE" ] || exit 1
git merge --ff-only "$TRK_BRANCH" || exit 1
```

`[check] verify` is an opaque shell command, exposed to templates as `verify`; empty means skip verification, not skip the worktree merge. Include dependency setup in the command when needed. Verification shares `[check] timeout` (configured `{{ config.check.timeout }}`) with the whole check and holds the serialized queue: use a fast subset or raise the timeout for slow suites. A failed verification, even exit 75, is a failure, not a retry. This local gate applies only when the issue type uses an issue worktree and branch; a type opting out has no issue branch to merge or test. The primary checkout receives only the verified result by fast-forward. The PR recipe below is unchanged by this setting.

Wait for a pull request (here number 12) to merge, waking only for base-branch conflicts, failed CI on the head commit, or closure without merging (query/authentication errors also fail). This uses `gh`'s built-in jq filter; `statusCheckRollup` contains the head commit's check runs and commit statuses:

```sh
result=$(gh pr view 12 --json state,baseRefName,mergeable,headRefOid,statusCheckRollup --jq '
  if .state == "MERGED" then "merged"
  elif .state == "CLOSED" then "PR closed without merging"
  elif .mergeable == "CONFLICTING" then "PR conflicts with base branch \(.baseRefName)"
  else
    .headRefOid as $head |
    [.statusCheckRollup[]? |
      select(
        (.__typename == "CheckRun" and .status == "COMPLETED" and
          (.conclusion | IN("FAILURE", "TIMED_OUT", "CANCELLED", "ACTION_REQUIRED", "STARTUP_FAILURE", "STALE"))) or
        (.__typename == "StatusContext" and (.state | IN("FAILURE", "ERROR")))
      ) |
      "\(.name // .context): \(.conclusion // .state) \(.detailsUrl // .targetUrl // "")"
    ] |
    if length > 0 then "CI failed on head \($head):\n" + join("\n")
    else "waiting" end
  end
') || exit "$?"
case "$result" in
  merged) exit 0 ;;
  waiting) exit 75 ;;
  *) printf "%s\n" "$result"; exit 1 ;;
esac
```

CI pending, awaiting review, unresolved threads, approved but not merged, and unknown mergeability all remain waiting (75) unless a failure above is present. Review threads and merging belong to the human unless they ask you to handle them.

A decision only a person can make has no check: call `wait` with just the `reason`, stating the question and the options. A human answers in a note and reopens or closes the issue.
